Subject: drm/nouveau/fifo/nv04: acknowledge RUNOUT instead of disabling it

NV_PFIFO_INTR_RUNOUT (bit 4) means the FIFO could not load a channel and
pushed the request into RAMRO. It was only ever handled under a
card_type == NV_50 test, so on nv04-family parts it fell through to the
catch-all at the end of nv04_fifo_intr():

	if (stat) {
		nvkm_warn(subdev, "intr %08x\n", stat);
		nvkm_mask(device, NV03_PFIFO_INTR_EN_0, stat, 0x00000000);
		nvkm_wr32(device, NV03_PFIFO_INTR_0, stat);
	}

which logs it once and then masks the interrupt off. NV03_PFIFO_INTR_EN_0 is
only ever written again at init, so RUNOUT stays disabled for the life of the
device and every later occurrence is silent.

Seen on a GeForce 7600 GS as a lone "fifo: intr 00000010", followed exactly
15 seconds later -- the fence deadline -- by a channel being declared dead.

Acknowledge it for every card type so the interrupt stays live and repeats
remain visible. RAMRO is already allocated on these parts (nv40.c), so a
future change can drain and report it properly.

NOTE: this is not confirmed to fix the stall. In the runs since, RUNOUT has
not recurred, so the patch has neither been exercised nor refuted. It is
included because masking an interrupt off permanently on first occurrence is
wrong on its own terms.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nvkm/engine/fifo/nv04.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nvkm/engine/fifo/nv04.c
@@ nv04_fifo_intr()
+	if (stat & NV_PFIFO_INTR_RUNOUT) {
+		stat &= ~NV_PFIFO_INTR_RUNOUT;
+		nvkm_wr32(device, NV03_PFIFO_INTR_0, NV_PFIFO_INTR_RUNOUT);
+		nvkm_error_ratelimited(subdev, "RUNOUT - ch %d\n", chid);
+	}
+
 	if (device->card_type == NV_50) {
-		if (stat & 0x00000010) {
-			stat &= ~0x00000010;
-			nvkm_wr32(device, 0x002100, 0x00000010);
-		}
-
 		if (stat & 0x40000000) {
